  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and unseasonably mild into mid-week with highs \nmainly in the upper 60s to the lower 70s. Some spots may reach into \nthe middle 70s for a day or two and challenge record highs. \n\nLittle change in the synoptic pattern as a stout upper ridge remains \nparked overhead through midweek. With ongoing antecedent dry \nconditions, leaned on the warmer envelope of guidance instead of the \nMOS/NBM output which continues to run a bit too cool for afternoon \nhighs. Highs will generally peak in the upper 60s to low 70s (cooler \nalong the immediate south coast, Cape, and Islands). With 925mb \ntemps warming to +11C to +15C, peak heating towards mid-week likely \npush locations in interior SNE to the mid 70s, likely putting \nseveral daily high records in jeopardy. Only real fly in the \nointment will be scattered high clouds periodically filtering the \nfull sun through mid-week. High confidence persists in an \nunseasonably mild, dry regime through Wednesday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Pattern becomes more unsettled late-week into the \nweekend featuring periodic chances for rain. \n\nEnsemble guidance shows a consensus in a shift of the pattern late \nThursday into Friday as a mid-level trough arrives from the west. \nThis will advect in a plume of rich moisture 2-3 standard deviations \nabove normal. This will be accompanied by a frontal system that \npushes across the Ohio River Valley and into the Mid-Atlantic and \nNortheast. This will likely bring us our next best shot at \nprecipitation. Details such as timing, rain amounts/rates are still \nlower confidence due to uncertainty in the tracking of the surface \nfeatures/best forcing. If the frontal features track closer, this \ncould put southern New England in better forced environment and \npotentially with marginal instability present. This could favor a \nfew rumbles of thunders or heavier downpours. Timing of this is low \nconfidence due to the track uncertainties, but the general timeframe \nis late Thu through Sat. Lighter/spotty showers may linger at \nlate as Sunday. However, it likely won't be rain for the entire \ntimeframe. \n\nEnsemble guidance highlights probabilities of 30-60% for rain totals \nof 0.50\" or more and low probabilities (10-30%) for 1.0\" or greater. \nDeterministic solutions have leaned toward the upper percentile \nsolutions of their ensemble suite showing potential for amounts 1.0 \nto upward of 2.0\" inches. Once guidance comes into better agreement, \nconfidence will increase in the exact timing of rainfall and totals. \n\nAbove normal temperatures are likely Thursday with the mid-level \nthermal ridge shifting over southern New England with 925mb \ntemperatures +13-15C. This will support high temperatures in the \nupper 60s to low/mid 70s! Temperatures likely lean seasonably warm \nfor Friday as well. Depending on how the system tracks, we may get \none more day of milder temperatures Saturday with more\nseasonable temperatures arriving by Sunday.",
